  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Elevated fire weather conditions today due to hot, dry, and \n   breezy conditions, especially in northwest Montana.\n\n-  Another surge of monsoon moisture is expected to arrive by\n   Tuesday, increasing cloud cover and thunderstorm chances.\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery show much drier air moving in from \nthe west, which will bring low relative humidity this afternoon. \nAlong with the dry airmass, a strengthening pressure gradient will\nbring increasing westerly winds across western Montana and north-\ncentral Idaho. This will translate to elevated fire weather \nconditions, especially across western Montana. Currently, there is\na 40 percent probability that wind gusts will range between 20 \nand 25 mph, with afternoon humidity values falling to 15 to 20 \npercent.\n\nOn Monday, a ridge of high pressure builds over the western U.S. and begins\nto pull up monsoon moisture from the southwest. Initially, the moisture\nwill bring mostly cloud cover. By Tuesday and Wednesday, available precipitable\nwater anomalies will rise to 200 to 250 percent of normal. This moisture,\ncoupled with an incoming shortwave trough, will initiate the development of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms, especially over west-central and\nsouthwest Montana, as well as north-central Idaho. These storms will bring\nperiods of strong, gusty, erratic winds, brief heavy rainfall, and frequent\nlightning. These storms will be similar to what the region experienced over\nthe past three days.\n\nBy the end of the week and next weekend, the center of the high pressure\nsystem will shift over the Northern Plains, opening the door to increasing\nwesterly winds in the middle of the atmosphere over the Northern Rockies.\nTemperatures will run 10 to 15 degrees above normal along with dry\nconditions.",
